{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply a downward level shift (≈ -0.2 to -0.8 €/t early horizon; widening to ≈ -2 to -6 €/t by days 15–30).",
    "Downtrend underestimation / lag: during sharp sell-offs the model reverts upward too quickly and fails to follow continued declines. Add a trend-following component (if last 10–15 days slope is negative, reduce the whole forward path proportionally to that slope).",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors grow with forecast horizon (convex error profile). Use a horizon penalty: subtract an increasing adjustment with lead time (e.g., linear or piecewise: small for days 1–7, moderate for 8–14, large for 15–30).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: the model pulls prices back toward a higher long-run mean than the market’s realized regime. Re-anchor mean reversion to a lower rolling mean (e.g., 10–20d average) when regime is bearish.",
    "Post-bounce overprojection: after short-lived rebounds in history, the model extrapolates recovery that doesn’t materialize. If recent history contains a rebound within an overall negative trend, cap upside drift and bias forecasts downward."
  ]
}
